  "notes": "Downloaded directly via BEA's public regional-data ZIP endpoint (no API key required): https://apps.bea.gov/regional/zip/MARPP.zip (table MARPP, 'Regional price parities by MSA', covering 2008-2024, 376 metro areas total in the raw file). Same index construction and LineCode mapping as us_rpp_state.json (1=all_items, 2=goods, 3=services_housing_rents [tenant+imputed-owner rents only, not owner cash outlays], 4=services_utilities, 5=services_other); see that file's notes for full detail. METRO SELECTION METHOD: BEA's MARPP file does not include population, so the 'top 50 metros' subset was selected by matching MSA-name keywords from an independent Census-based 2025 population ranking (https://statranker.org/population/u-s-metro-areas-by-population-2026-snapshot-based-on-2025-census-estimates/, itself sourced from Census Bureau July 2025 metro population estimates) against BEA's GeoName field via substring matching, since BEA's MSA delineations/names in this file may differ slightly in vintage from the most current Census delineations (OMB periodically revises MSA definitions and BEA lags). All 50 target metros were successfully matched. 'match_keyword' field on each row records which population-ranking search term matched it, for traceability/debugging -- it is not part of the core schema and can be dropped by the frontend. No dollar-denominated cost-of-living figures are available from BEA RPP for metros either -- purely a relative price-level index requiring a dollar anchor from another source to convert to currency terms."
}